WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:12.500 --> 00:00:13.280
[Man is seated in dental chair with paper
drape over his torso up to his neck.]

00:00:13.290 --> 00:00:14.080
[Nurse:] How was your trip?

00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:15.339
[Mr.Jensen:] Oh, wonderful.

00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:17.660
[Nurse:] Did your wife enjoy it?

00:00:17.670 --> 00:00:18.730
[Mr. Jensen:] Very much.

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:24.480
[Nurse:] Oh, that's good. Excuse me, now Mr. Jensen, and Dr. Kwapis will be right in.

00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:26.400
[Mr. Jensen:] Thank you.

00:00:26.860 --> 00:00:28.340
[Dentist:] Well, Mr. Jensen, how are you today?

00:00:28.520 --> 00:00:29.960
[Mr. Jensen:] Fine, doctor, how are you?

00:00:30.100 --> 00:00:34.380
[Dentist:] I'll get this up just a little
bit. That's better. That's good.

00:00:34.480 --> 00:00:38.480
Now, let's check your, this again, Mr. Jensen.

00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:42.760
[Narrator:] The mouth is the most easily examined of all body openings, 

00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:47.240
yet there is a tendency for dentists to focus on the teeth and gingiva 

00:00:47.380 --> 00:00:50.540
and for physicians to look through the mouth to observe the throat.

00:00:51.260 --> 00:00:54.900
Systematic examination of the oral cavity is too often neglected.

00:00:55.820 --> 00:00:58.560
Twice each year, with every regular patient,

00:00:58.640 --> 00:01:02.100
the dentist has an opportunity to search for early cancer.

00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:03.220
[Dentist examines Mr. Jensen's mouth.] 

00:01:03.220 --> 00:01:07.920
[Dentist:] Now close together. Now open wide, please.

00:01:08.140 --> 00:01:11.060
Hmm, the biopsy site seems to have healed nicely.

00:01:11.060 --> 00:01:13.420
[Dentist pulls back patient's lower lip to examine the area.]

00:01:13.580 --> 00:01:17.740
[Mr. Jensen:] That sounds encouraging. Dr.
Toleman suggested that white spot be removed.

00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:22.620
[Dentist:] Dr. Toleman is a first-rate specialist and I'd certainly follow his advice.

00:01:22.630 --> 00:01:23.920
What did he say about the pipe smoking?

00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:25.819
[Mr. Jensen:] He suggests I discontinue.

00:01:25.819 --> 00:01:28.200
[Dentist:] Well, this would aggravate your
problem.

00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:31.400
[Mr. Jensen:] Yeah, I know but it would be
hard to do.

00:01:31.800 --> 00:01:36.000
[Dentist inserts patient's lower denture.]

00:01:36.140 --> 00:01:39.269
[Dentist:] Now, close together.

00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:47.220
[Narrator:] Many dentists perform a checkup
for oral cancer routinely on all patients.

00:01:47.229 --> 00:01:53.200
Although relatively uncommon, representing
only one cancer in 30, oral cancer so often

00:01:53.200 --> 00:01:57.080
is a killer that persistent search is required.

00:01:57.780 --> 00:02:01.240
Except for the lip, the five-year survival rate is less than 40 percent,

00:02:01.660 --> 00:02:04.520
[People are in a waiting room, and a graph
is displayed atop that image.] 

00:02:04.740 --> 00:02:08.280
But when treatment takes place before the cancer has spread, 

00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:13.880
survival is several times as high as when lymph nodes are involved.

00:02:14.920 --> 00:02:18.920
Absence of spread
depends on very early detection.

00:02:19.720 --> 00:02:23.820
In one study of tongue cancer, 40 percent had metastasized 

00:02:23.880 --> 00:02:26.660
within three months after the first symptoms were noticed.

00:02:26.719 --> 00:02:28.939
[Another graph is displayed over patients
in waiting area.] 

00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:31.580
After 12 months, 90 percent had metastasized.

00:02:37.840 --> 00:02:41.379
[Dr. Nathaniel H. Rowe, DDS:] Oral cancer
is largely a disease of men, who outnumber

00:02:41.379 --> 00:02:44.200
the women affected by about three to one.

00:02:44.780 --> 00:02:50.120
This may be due to men's greater
exposure to various etiologic factors such

00:02:50.120 --> 00:02:56.340
as sunlight, tobacco, alcohol.

00:02:57.720 --> 00:02:59.360
[An older man with a cancer of the mouth is
shown.] 

00:02:59.360 --> 00:03:03.940
All of these factors manifest their cancerous effect increasingly with advancing age. 

00:03:05.980 --> 00:03:10.560
Squamous cell carcinomas, sometimes called epidermoid carcinomas,

00:03:10.700 --> 00:03:15.120
 account for about eight out of ten oral cancers. 

00:03:15.120 --> 00:03:23.300
Together with cancers reported merely as carcinoma, they make up more than 90 percent of the total.

00:03:25.340 --> 00:03:32.101
Non-ulcerated soft tissue cancers usually arise from major or minor salivary glands. 

00:03:32.180 --> 00:03:37.260
These comprise about seven percent of all oral cancers. 

00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:43.380
Sarcomas and lymphomas, while rare, are particularly noteworthy because they occur in younger patients.

00:03:43.600 --> 00:03:48.020
[A young blond boy is shown, then a close-up of a tongue with white patches.] 

00:03:48.020 --> 00:03:52.140
Squamous cell carcinoma does not appear to originate in a single cell, 

00:03:52.140 --> 00:03:56.840
but in an area of epithelium which undergoes cancerous change at many points.

00:03:57.660 --> 00:04:02.960
These foci then coalesce to produce a single tumor. 

00:04:04.540 --> 00:04:08.800
Local growth is followed by invasion of underlying tissue,

00:04:09.040 --> 00:04:13.620
which often spreads
much farther than the clinical evidence would suggest.

00:04:15.820 --> 00:04:20.600
In the wake of invasion comes metastasis,
initially to the lymph nodes of the neck.

00:04:21.989 --> 00:04:27.529
In one study, some 40 percent of oral cancers already involved lymph nodes 

00:04:27.580 --> 00:04:29.300
by the time they were diagnosed.

00:04:29.300 --> 00:04:31.580
[The neck and chin of a patient are shown,
with a hand palpating the neck area.]

00:04:31.590 --> 00:04:38.590
Neglect and further growth means eventual death by strangulation, starvation, or hemorrhage.

00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:47.220
[Dr. Rowe:] Cancer in different oral sites
presents different problems.

00:04:47.580 --> 00:04:51.040
The lower lip is by far the most frequent site. 

00:04:51.220 --> 00:04:54.740
However, lip cancer develops where it is likely to be seen early.

00:04:54.750 --> 00:05:01.750
And the five-year survival rate is high; at least 70 percent.

00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:05.620
In contrast, cancers of the tongue and of the floor of the mouth 

00:05:05.700 --> 00:05:07.880
are seldom detected in an early stage.

00:05:07.890 --> 00:05:09.370
[A diseased tongue is manipulated.]

00:05:09.460 --> 00:05:12.620
And they cause more deaths than any other oral cancers.

00:05:12.620 --> 00:05:19.620
At present, the five-year survival rate for
intra-oral cancer is only about 30 percent.

00:05:20.320 --> 00:05:27.320
This carcinoma with its abrupt border, induration, ulceration, and a history of duration and growth

00:05:27.780 --> 00:05:33.120
would hardly be missed by any practitioner,
but this is not early cancer.

00:05:34.960 --> 00:05:39.940
Improved survival will come only
when cancer is detected before these classical

00:05:39.950 --> 00:05:43.350
signs appear, before it looks like cancer.

00:05:43.350 --> 00:05:46.160
[A woman is seated in a dental chair, and
the dental hygienist approaches her.]

00:05:46.160 --> 00:05:49.230
[Nurse:] I'll take the Kleenex, Mrs. Nagle.

00:05:49.230 --> 00:05:54.740
[Dentist:] Before I examine your teeth, Mrs.
Nagle, I'd like to ask you some questions.

00:05:54.740 --> 00:05:55.900
Have you ever had bleeding gums?

00:05:55.900 --> 00:05:56.730
[Mrs. Nagle:] No.

00:05:56.730 --> 00:06:00.670
[Dentist:] Have you ever had any sores, ulcers,
or swellings that haven't healed promptly?

00:06:00.670 --> 00:06:01.990
[Mrs. Nagle:] No.

00:06:01.990 --> 00:06:08.310
[Narrator:] Taking a history of oral conditions
is the first step in a routine examination.

00:06:08.310 --> 00:06:16.030
Equipment for examination: a mouth mirror,
gauze sponge, good illumination, 

00:06:16.960 --> 00:06:24.540
and a high degree of concern about cancer. A systematic examination sequence is essential. 

00:06:25.920 --> 00:06:31.060
The dentist looks for any asymmetry or swelling and for changes in skin color or texture.

00:06:31.070 --> 00:06:32.590
[The dentist turns Mrs. Nagle's head and begins to examine her.]

00:06:32.660 --> 00:06:36.140
By watching for neck masses, salivary gland tumors,

00:06:36.220 --> 00:06:42.440
thyroid enlargement, and skin cancer,
he may reap a bonus in case-finding.

00:06:43.440 --> 00:06:49.920
He searches the lips for an area of induration, elevation, or other surface irregularity.

00:06:49.920 --> 00:06:53.300
[The patient's lip is pulled and manipulated.]

00:06:53.420 --> 00:06:59.120
Removing dentures will reveal lesions, which may otherwise be obscured.

00:07:01.940 --> 00:07:06.980
Drying the mucosa aids visualizing variations in color and texture. 

00:07:09.140 --> 00:07:14.940
He palpates the buccal mucosa to note any lump, fixation, or induration. 

00:07:16.680 --> 00:07:18.260
The gingiva...

00:07:18.440 --> 00:07:35.180
[The dentist examines and palpates the gums.]

00:07:39.040 --> 00:07:40.280
The palate...

00:07:40.320 --> 00:07:43.460
[The dentist checks the roof of the patient's mouth.]

00:07:43.460 --> 00:07:45.460
The tongue.

00:07:45.460 --> 00:07:50.800
Grasping the tongue with a sponge aids him in examining the obscure posterior lateral borders 

00:07:50.900 --> 00:07:57.540
of the tongue and tonsillar pillars where the likelihood of finding an early cancer is greatest. 

00:07:57.900 --> 00:08:03.060
Using a mouth mirror as tongue depressor makes obscure areas more visible.

00:08:03.070 --> 00:08:05.580
[Dentist:] Say ah, please.

00:08:05.580 --> 00:08:07.320
[Mrs. Nagle:] Ahhh.

00:08:07.320 --> 00:08:13.590
[Narrator:] This drops the tongue and raises the soft palate and uvula, revealing the tonsils,

00:08:13.590 --> 00:08:17.710
fosses, and post-pharyngeal wall. 

00:08:19.540 --> 00:08:24.840
Checking for swelling or lumps, he palpates the salivary glands. 

00:08:26.240 --> 00:08:30.600
Palpation practiced routinely extends the dentist's perceptive senses;

00:08:30.840 --> 00:08:33.560
gives him educated fingertips. 

00:08:34.000 --> 00:08:40.600
He palpates the neck too, and the cervical lymph nodes which drain the oral region.

00:08:40.660 --> 00:08:44.920
Sometimes an enlarged lymph
node may be the first sign detected,

00:08:45.040 --> 00:08:48.660
before the primary lesion has become apparent. 

00:08:48.740 --> 00:08:55.740
When infection is not the obvious cause, enlarged lymph nodes suggest cancer until proven otherwise.

00:08:55.749 --> 00:08:59.139
[Dentist:] Now let me examine your teeth.

00:09:00.680 --> 00:09:07.780
[Narrator:] People tend to be unaware of early oral cancer because it is usually painless and presents no handicap.

00:09:08.860 --> 00:09:12.840
The dentist's greatest opportunity for salvage lies with his regular patients

00:09:12.850 --> 00:09:17.660
in finding the early lesions that
one might not suspect of being cancer.

00:09:17.660 --> 00:09:19.720
[A patient's tongue is explored with a narrow tongue depressor.] 

00:09:19.780 --> 00:09:23.180
This velvety red area on the tongue is cancer.

00:09:23.180 --> 00:09:27.900
Any change in color or texture like this should arouse suspicion.

00:09:29.140 --> 00:09:33.300
This whitish area on the palate lies adjacent to a carcinoma, 

00:09:35.060 --> 00:09:42.060
but this equally suspicious milk-white raised lesion is not cancer. 

00:09:42.060 --> 00:09:46.240
Biopsy is essential to differentiate benign from malignant.

00:09:48.300 --> 00:09:53.820
Any lesion which persists should
be biopsied. This one proved benign.

00:09:55.680 --> 00:10:03.180
Early carcinoma of the lip may resemble common lesions such as cigarette burn, senile elastosis,

00:10:03.980 --> 00:10:07.260
or chronic cracking and fissuring. 

00:10:07.260 --> 00:10:11.340
The history of duration may help to determine how it should be regarded.

00:10:11.720 --> 00:10:14.840
This one is cancer. 

00:10:15.400 --> 00:10:19.140
Here one cannot observe the highlights, which are visible on nearby tissues.

00:10:20.380 --> 00:10:24.980
Any roughened or atrophic mucosa with altered translucency may be cancer.

00:10:25.120 --> 00:10:28.820
[Examples of mouth conditions both benign
and malignant are shown.]

00:10:29.100 --> 00:10:32.460
This tiny ulcer could easily be mistaken for denture injury. 

00:10:33.120 --> 00:10:36.160
It proved to be a small squamous cell carcinoma.

00:10:38.140 --> 00:10:43.740
This lesion with its papillary, warty surface
is verrucous carcinoma. 

00:10:45.040 --> 00:10:47.680
This lesion also is non-ulcerated. 

00:10:48.040 --> 00:10:52.320
Being small in size and located posterior to the denture-bearing area,

00:10:52.380 --> 00:10:56.280
it might easily have been overlooked. 

00:10:56.900 --> 00:11:01.800
Diffuse in its distribution, this cancer mimicked infectious disease.

00:11:01.840 --> 00:11:04.240
[The mouth is probed to demonstrate the areas and lesions described.]

00:11:04.480 --> 00:11:09.020
Whether ulcerated or not, any undiagnosed lesion of more than

00:11:09.020 --> 00:11:12.280
ten days duration should be biopsied. 

00:11:12.540 --> 00:11:18.680
Watchful waiting while treating only the symptoms may prove fatal to the patient.

00:11:20.280 --> 00:11:23.760
In short, anything unusual may be cancer. 

00:11:24.640 --> 00:11:34.480
Any increase in size, any color difference, any persistent ache or pain, any erosion.

00:11:37.240 --> 00:11:38.540
Any change. 

00:11:39.640 --> 00:11:42.780
The future of the person who does have cancer

00:11:42.780 --> 00:11:45.640
lies largely in the hands of the first practitioner 

00:11:45.820 --> 00:11:48.560
who has the opportunity to initiate action. 

00:11:49.080 --> 00:11:54.780
Because time is so critical, often meaning the difference between life and death,

00:11:54.920 --> 00:11:59.380
the dentist should refer the patient
to a specialist who is skilled in the management

00:11:59.460 --> 00:12:02.960
of cancer to assure the best possible treatment.

00:12:04.560 --> 00:12:07.580
[Dentist:] Mrs. Huff, would you get Dr. Walker
on the phone, please?

00:12:07.720 --> 00:12:09.290
[Nurse:] Yes, doctor.

00:12:09.290 --> 00:12:15.119
[Dentist:] Mrs. Harn, you have a small spot
on your palate that needs special examination.

00:12:15.119 --> 00:12:19.079
It may require a small sample to be removed
for microscopic study. 

00:12:20.320 --> 00:12:22.200
I'd like to have you see a Dr. Walker tomorrow.

00:12:22.500 --> 00:12:24.180
 Could you arrange that, please?

00:12:25.040 --> 00:12:28.040
[Mrs. Harn:] I guess so, but does it have
to be so soon?

00:12:28.040 --> 00:12:32.899
[Dentist:] Well, usually such conditions are
minor, but there is a possibility that it

00:12:32.899 --> 00:12:36.040
could need quick attention and we really need
to find out.

00:12:36.040 --> 00:12:38.759
[Nurse:] I have Dr. Walker on the phone, sir.

00:12:38.759 --> 00:12:46.979
[Dentist:] Thank you, Mrs. Huff. Thank you.
Hello? Dr. Walker? This is Dr. Osterkamp speaking.

00:12:47.009 --> 00:12:50.949
I have a patient here with a lesion of the
palate I'd like you to check.

00:12:51.220 --> 00:12:58.660
Her name is Mrs. Willis Harn. Could you possibly see her tomorrow? At 2:30?

00:12:59.900 --> 00:13:03.400
[Male student:] Why doesn't he just take a
biopsy specimen himself? He's qualified.

00:13:03.400 --> 00:13:04.760
[Dr. Howe stands at a lectern in a classroom.]

00:13:04.760 --> 00:13:10.400
[Dr. Howe:] Yes, he is, but what about that? When might a dentist take the biopsy himself

00:13:10.500 --> 00:13:11.920
and when might he refer it?

00:13:11.920 --> 00:13:16.459
[Mr. Richter:] Well, if he feels that it is
a cancer, he should send his patient right

00:13:16.459 --> 00:13:21.240
to the specialist who will treat him. Then
the lesion won't get any unnecessary handling.

00:13:21.240 --> 00:13:24.940
[Dr.Howe:] Fine. Any other ideas?

00:13:25.300 --> 00:13:30.020
[Male student:] Well, whenever the nature
of the lesion is uncertain, it should be biopsied.

00:13:30.160 --> 00:13:34.680
Patients often tend to procrastinate, so the
biopsy is best done by the dentist

00:13:34.840 --> 00:13:37.480
while the patient is still seated.

00:13:38.580 --> 00:13:43.340
[Dr.Howe:] Good. What does this leave for
explorative cytology? Mr. Richter?

00:13:43.440 --> 00:13:48.520
[Mr. Richter:] I'd say even more than before, because if suspicion is upgraded, so are all the tests.

00:13:49.260 --> 00:13:54.120
Explorative cytology should be
done when clinical judgment wouldn't indicate biopsy.

00:13:55.100 --> 00:13:59.860
[Male student:] But the surface scraping test can leave a deep-seated cancer undetected,

00:13:59.869 --> 00:14:02.490
especially if it is covered with leukoplakia.

00:14:03.520 --> 00:14:11.340
[Dr. Howe:] True, and cytology appears limited to finding only one type of cancer, epidermoid carcinoma.

00:14:12.840 --> 00:14:19.180
Keep in mind that neither a negative
cytology, nor even a negative biopsy, rules out cancer.

00:14:19.680 --> 00:14:25.220
It simply means that cancer cells were not seen in the specimen submitted.

00:14:25.740 --> 00:14:30.760
If the condition does not behave as you believe it should, it should be checked again.

00:14:34.220 --> 00:14:36.520
[Dr. Osterkamp sits at his desk, making a phone call.]

00:14:36.520 --> 00:14:40.420
[Dr. Osterkamp:] Hello, Dr. Walker? Did Mrs. Harn see you for the biopsy?

00:14:40.580 --> 00:14:46.200
[Dr. Walker]: Yes, she did. I just got the
report. It shows verrucous carcinoma.

00:14:46.440 --> 00:14:50.640
Fortunately, this is very amenable to surgery. We'll plan her operation in the morning.

00:14:50.720 --> 00:14:52.400
[Mrs. Harn wears a blue dress and is seated
on an examining table in a medical office.]

00:14:52.410 --> 00:14:57.470
[Narrator:] When biopsy confirms the presence of cancer, how is treatment determined?

00:14:57.470 --> 00:14:59.910
What are the principles that govern therapy?

00:15:00.440 --> 00:15:02.620
[John S. Pratt, Jr., M.D., Ellis Fischel State
Cancer Hospital:] The treatment for a cancer

00:15:02.660 --> 00:15:09.020
is determined by its location, its extent,
its pathological characteristics,

00:15:09.120 --> 00:15:13.860
and by knowledge of its natural history or behavior.

00:15:14.240 --> 00:15:17.559
Has experience shown that a particular cancer is best treated

00:15:17.559 --> 00:15:24.559
by radiotherapy, or surgery, or by a combination of methods?

00:15:24.560 --> 00:15:33.540
If surgery is selected, the extent will be governed by the need to remove involved tissues with an adequate margin.

00:15:33.780 --> 00:15:37.860
If the surgeon doesn't remove it, the cancer will. 

00:15:38.000 --> 00:15:43.920
Fortunately, early oral cancer seldom spreads beyond the lymph nodes of the neck.

00:15:44.840 --> 00:15:49.620
Even when the cervical lymph nodes are involved, cure is often possible.

00:15:51.279 --> 00:15:57.249
When radiotherapy is selected, it must be
equally radical and expert to eliminate cancer

00:15:57.249 --> 00:16:04.029
without excessively damaging normal tissues or the functions of organs or the patient's appearance.

00:16:05.640 --> 00:16:13.040
For some conditions, the coordinated
use of surgery and radiotherapy may be the most beneficial.

00:16:13.120 --> 00:16:17.880
This requires teamwork by the pathologist, the surgeon, and the radiologist.

00:16:18.560 --> 00:16:23.680
The prosthodontist should participate when
the treatment will result in an intra-oral defect.

00:16:24.640 --> 00:16:30.220
When there is no indication of spread,
the treatment can be less radical.

00:16:30.230 --> 00:16:36.040
When more head and neck cancers are detected early, primary lesions can be controlled with

00:16:36.040 --> 00:16:41.249
fewer disabling operations and with a favorable prognosis for more patients.

00:16:41.249 --> 00:16:43.220
[Woman lies in hospital bed after surgery.]

00:16:43.220 --> 00:16:48.800
After a cancer is treated, frequent follow-up examinations are imperative.

00:16:49.020 --> 00:16:52.480
 Even a small lesion may metastasize, but this spread

00:16:52.489 --> 00:16:55.600
may not be detectable for some time.

00:16:55.600 --> 00:17:01.060
[Dr. Howe:] Moreover, the person who has had
oral cancer may develop

00:17:01.060 --> 00:17:05.700
additional primary tumors or local recurrence or metastases.

00:17:06.660 --> 00:17:11.200
[Narrator:] The patient must be taught to
participate in his follow-up by reporting

00:17:11.209 --> 00:17:17.269
anything unusual without delay and by keeping
his appointments for periodic re-examination.

00:17:19.540 --> 00:17:23.779
[Dr. Howe:] Mrs. Harn's dentist was able to
arrange early treatment because Mrs. Harn

00:17:23.779 --> 00:17:28.779
belongs to the 40 percent of our population
that gets regular dental care.

00:17:28.920 --> 00:17:34.760
Improved public education is one means by which we may reach the other 60 percent. 

00:17:34.920 --> 00:17:40.820
True, the person who realizes there is something wrong with his mouth will very often go to a dentist,

00:17:40.820 --> 00:17:44.240
but he may take a dangerous amount of time getting around to it. 

00:17:44.560 --> 00:17:53.840
In one study, the average patient with oral cancer put off seeing anyone until eight months after he noticed a lesion.

00:17:54.160 --> 00:17:59.040
When the typical patient has it checked, it is no longer early. 

00:17:59.180 --> 00:18:07.660
However, the dentist can still play a role in salvage by seeing that his patient receives immediate and proper referral.

00:18:08.460 --> 00:18:13.200
He can also prepare study models
before cancer treatment, so that when a prosthesis is made,

00:18:13.210 --> 00:18:18.750
it will closely restore the patient's
normal appearance. What procedures are now

00:18:18.750 --> 00:18:24.310
in store for the cancer patient whose disease has spread beyond the primary site?

00:18:24.960 --> 00:18:29.380
Many of these cases call for immediate radical surgery and neck dissection.

00:18:29.540 --> 00:18:34.320
[Surgery is shown.] 

00:18:34.660 --> 00:18:38.380
If the surgeon doesn't remove it, the cancer will.

00:18:40.080 --> 00:18:43.900
End results of such procedures are very gratifying, 

00:18:44.020 --> 00:18:48.780
but the patient whose life is preserved may wonder for a while if it's worth it. 

00:18:50.060 --> 00:18:55.120
Functional impairment
and disfiguration lead to depression and hopelessness.

00:18:56.350 --> 00:19:01.250
The prosthodontist who is called in beforehand
can often provide an appliance within a week

00:19:01.250 --> 00:19:07.330
after surgery. This will lessen the contraction
of soft tissue and what it does for the patient's

00:19:07.330 --> 00:19:12.470
ability to feed himself, to swallow and to
speak, will once again give him hope.

00:19:15.520 --> 00:19:22.880
[Man in dental chair attempts to speak, but
words are not clear.]

00:19:23.010 --> 00:19:27.390
[Narrator:] Treatment today is providing oral cancer patients with much more than

00:19:27.400 --> 00:19:29.919
just greater life expectancy.

00:19:31.020 --> 00:19:33.180
[Doctor:] What this prosthesis means
to you?

00:19:33.190 --> 00:19:37.690
[Man:] It means my very life, doctor. Without
these, I might as well be dead.

00:19:37.690 --> 00:19:45.220
I can't live without them. I can't eat, drink, I can't talk. It is my life; that's what it is.

00:19:47.960 --> 00:19:51.480
[A man looks in the mirror and inserts a prosthesis,]

00:19:51.760 --> 00:19:55.620
[Narrator:] Dentists can save thousands of
patients every year, 

00:19:55.640 --> 00:20:00.400
and thereby ensure for them a meaningful extension of life.

00:20:00.500 --> 00:20:09.440
[People seated around a table with drinks
and food talk to one another.]

